  "pli-tv-bu-vb-np26:2.9": "Sp 1.636: <i lang='pi' translate='no'>Sāṇanti sāṇavākasuttaṁ. Bhaṅganti pāṭekkaṁ vākasuttamevāti eke. Etehi pañcahi missetvā katasuttaṁ pana “bhaṅga”nti veditabbaṁ</i>, “<i lang='pi' translate='no'>Sāṇa</i>: thread from the bark of hemp. <i lang='pi' translate='no'>Bhaṅga</i>: some say it is just a separate thread from bark. But <i lang='pi' translate='no'>bhaṅga</i> is to be understood as the thread made by mixing the (other) five.” SED says: “<i lang='pi' translate='no'>śaṇa</i>, m. (L. also n.) a kind of hemp, Cannabis Sativa or Crotolaria Juncea …” And: “<i lang='pi' translate='no'>bhāṅga</i>, mf(<i lang='pi' translate='no'>ī</i>)n, (fr. <i lang='pi' translate='no'>bhaṅgā</i>) hempen, made or consisting of hemp …” and “Bhaṅgā, f. hemp (Cannabis Sativa); an intoxicating beverage (or narcotic drug commonly called ‘bhang’) prepared from the hemp plant”. Since SED identifies <i lang='pi' translate='no'>bhaṅga</i> with Cannabis sativa, I take <i lang='pi' translate='no'>sāṇa</i> to be Crotolaria juncea, otherwise known as “sunn hemp”. ",
